The paper "A Business by Any Other Name: Firm Name Choice as a Signal of Firm Quality" by Ryan C. McDevitt examines the strategic selection of firm names as a signal of firm quality, particularly within the plumbing industry. Key findings indicate that firms whose names begin with 'A' or a number receive five times more service complaints than those with other names, despite also charging higher prices. These firms are more prevalent in larger markets and invest more in advertising.

Key methodologies include examining data from various sources like the Better Business Bureau, Dun & Bradstreet, Angie’s List, Google, and Yelp to analyze complaint frequencies and advertising impacts. The results show that firms with 'A' names receive more complaints, regardless of business volume or customer composition, evidenced by their similar sales-to-employee ratios and poor ratings on Angie’s List and Yelp.

The study concludes that the choice of an 'A' name is a deliberate signal designed to attract less discerning customers with lower search costs. This naming strategy seems correlated with low-quality service, as evidenced by a significant increase in complaints. Furthermore, these firms often charge an 8.4% price premium. The prevalence of such name choices and the extent of quality dispersion increase with market size, suggesting low-quality firms use specific names to target uninformed consumers.

In contrast to theories suggesting costly advertising signals quality, this study finds that firms spending more on advertising, such as Yellow Pages or Google, tend to receive more complaints. This contrasts with theoretical models predicting that high-quality firms bid for better positions in auctions to match with consumers effectively.

Overall, McDevitt's paper contributes to understanding how firm names function as signals of quality 
